    //  2 Which model are you running?
    //  3 What does the sub have to cover?
    //  4 How do you work the number out?
    //  5 What if attendance drops?
    //  6 Short of members, or short of courts?
    //  7 What should a guest session cost?
    //  8 How deep should concessions go?
    //  9 Should the club hold a surplus?
    // 10 If you are a CASC, is there a ceiling?
    // 11 How do you put subs up?
    // 12 Should you publish the price at all?

    var faqPairs = new (string Q, string A)[]
    {
        ("Can we charge different amounts on different nights?",
         "Yes, and clubs do it where the nights differ in cost or demand. The complication is administrative rather than principled: two prices means two things to explain, two things to collect and two things to get wrong. Most clubs that try it end up folding it back into one rate."),
        ("Should juniors pay a share of the adult sub, or a separate rate?",
         "A separate rate, almost always, because junior sessions usually have their own court cost and their own coaching. Working out the junior number the same way you worked out the adult one is more defensible than picking a percentage of it."),
        ("What if two clubs share a hall - can we share the affiliation too?",
         "No. Affiliation is per club, and its insurance cover follows the club that holds it. Sharing a venue does not make you one club, and a shared arrangement that assumes it will leave one of you uncovered."),
        ("Do we have to charge the same as the club down the road?",
         "No, and the survey suggests nobody does. Clubs a few miles apart run different models entirely. What you are competing on is the night, not the price, and members who leave over a ten-pound difference were usually leaving anyway."),
        ("Is a joining fee a good idea?",
         "It is rare in badminton and we found none in the survey. It suppresses exactly the thing most clubs want, which is people trying it. If you need to raise more, raising the sub is simpler and reads better."),
