      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      @foreach (var f in faqPairs)
      {
        <h3>@f.Q</h3>
        <p>@f.A</p>
      }

      <h2 id="related">Where to go next</h2>
      <p>Setting the number is half of it. <a href="/guides/collecting-club-subs">Collecting club subs</a> deals with the other half, and its finding is that the payment method matters far less than how often you bill.</p>
      <p>The largest line in the arithmetic above is the hall, and it has more give in it than most clubs realise: <a href="/guides/badminton-court-hire">what UK clubs pay for court hire</a> covers the survey and the levers. The second largest is shuttles, and <a href="/guides/feather-vs-nylon-shuttlecocks">feather vs nylon</a> works out the cost per session.</p>
      <p>If you are setting subs because you are starting from scratch, <a href="/guides/how-to-start-a-badminton-club">how to start a badminton club</a> covers the rest of it.</p>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">How this guide was put together</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">ePegboard builds club-night software. We do not collect subs, we are not accountants, and we have never set a club's fees. The figures here come from our own survey: we probed 923 UK club websites on 26 August 2026, read by hand every page that stated a price, and verified 41 as a badminton club publishing its own fees. Alongside that sit Badminton England's affiliation prices and the government's published CASC guidance.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0">Three honest limits. The 41 clubs are those that published a readable price, not a random sample of clubs - clubs that publish tend to be the ones recruiting, and a club at capacity has no reason to say anything. The set is England-heavy, with a few Welsh and Scottish clubs and none from Northern Ireland. We found no UK benchmark for club reserves, so that section gives a method rather than a figure. And we found no evidence at all on how members react to an increase, so we have described what clubs do structurally and left their reactions alone. Individual Badminton England membership is quoted at more than one price depending on route, and we could not establish which applies to an ordinary club member, so no figure for it appears above.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.badmintonengland.co.uk/membership/club-affiliation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Badminton England club affiliation</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/community-amateur-sports-clubs-detailed-guidance-notes/community-amateur-sports-clubs-detailed-guidance-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HMRC, Community Amateur Sports Clubs detailed guidance</a> &middot; <a href="https://buddle.co/learning-and-support-resources/money-matters/raising-money/membership-options" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Buddle on membership options</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.hertsbadminton.net/membership-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hertfordshire Badminton Association fee breakdown</a></p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.9rem;margin:12px 0 0">Published 26 August 2026 by ePegboard. Club fees read 26 August 2026; most clubs reset in August or September.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
